    id: "yibin-slide",
    title: "Silver Flash // 70m Slide",
    year: "2025",
    location: "Yibin, Sichuan",
    client: "Yibin MixC World (宜宾万象天地)",
    role: "Lead Creative Concept & Structural Execution",
    hook: "An elevated mirror stainless steel lightning slide cutting across green hills with dual elevation bridges, astronomical night lighting, and an automated closed-loop safety gate system.",
    background: "Yibin MixC World Green Core Park required an outdoor civic landmark for youth demographics—a participatory, highly photographed social anchor within urban public space. The terrain consisted of an elevated grass slope lacking sufficient natural inclination for a gravity slide. The challenge demanded achieving a safe 25° sliding trajectory while managing high-altitude metal component safety, weather constraints, and daily operational maintenance.",
    whatIDid: "Adopting 'Silver Lightning' as the design motif, I designed an elevated mirror stainless steel slide suspended above the grassy knoll that serves as both an amusement facility and a bold kinetic art sculpture. To overcome the slope deficit, the trajectory is elevated twice: an entrance portal rising 4.2m and an aerial skybridge rising 3.5m, creating lookout resting nodes while securing the 25° incline. The entrance features folded angular facets evoking lightning descending from the sky, countered by radiating beam details at the landing basin. At night, programmable LED strips deliver three dynamic light sequences: 'Meteor' plunging from the crest, 'Pulsing' halos breathing across the portals, and 'Liquid River' flowing in orange-pink twilight gradients. To ensure operational safety, I engineered a closed-loop flow: QR code scan & waiver -> slide suit checkout -> ride -> exit infrared sensor verification -> suit return -> gate reactivation, backed by weather protocols and CCTV surveillance.",
    outcome: "Fully deployed and operational as a dual-function day-to-night civic public art landmark. The project has welcomed 3,000–5,000 visitors with weekend peaks reaching 200–300 riders per day, establishing a reusable operational blueprint for high-altitude metal slides.",
